WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.433 --> 00:00:02.802
-You know
our first guest tonight

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.370
from films such as "Hustlers"
and "Crazy Rich Asians"

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:05.938
and shows like
"Fresh Off the Boat"

00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:07.540
and "The Terminal List."

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.976
Her book, "Making a Scene"
is on sale now,

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:11.677
and she stars in
"Lyle, Lyle, Crocodile,"

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:13.112
which is in theaters everywhere
this Friday.

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:15.515
Please welcome back to the show,
Constance Wu, everybody!

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:18.284
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:23.189 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:28.094 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:32.999 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.767
-Hello, Constance!
-Hi!

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.569
-I'm so happy you're back.
-You know, I didn't know Fred

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:38.738
was here, and I have
to just tell Fred --

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:39.905
-Please.

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:42.642
-When I first booked
my first network sitcom,

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:45.278
"Fresh Off the Boat,"
I had no experience with comedy,

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:48.147
so I said,
"I gotta study somebody."

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:49.982
And you are who I studied
to learn how to be funny.

00:00:49.982 --> 00:00:52.619
-Aww.
-How'd I do?

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:54.086
-That's so nice.
-I did!

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:55.522
-Thank you very much.
-Yeah.

00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:57.390
[ Applause ]

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.025
-Now, knowing Fred,
he's gonna ask for a commission,

00:01:00.025 --> 00:01:01.060
now that you've said that.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.564
-$400 a month, in installments.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.098
-Ooh! It paid off.

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:08.234
-Learn from the best.

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:12.405
-So this is a book of
personal essays.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.106
-Yes.
-Which must have been,

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:16.842
I would imagine, challenging,
cathartic to write?

00:01:16.842 --> 00:01:20.312
You talk about how acting
came to you very young.

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:21.681
-Mm-hmm.
-You share a photo.

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:24.317
You were the Mole in
"Wind in the Willows"?

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.418
-Yes, my first leading role.

00:01:26.418 --> 00:01:27.754
-This is
your first leading role.

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:31.624
Look at you.
A very convincing mole.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.325
-The Jeremy Strong of moles.

00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:35.594
-[ Laughter ] Yeah. You lived
like a mole for a whole...

00:01:35.594 --> 00:01:38.097
-No, if you look at
that picture, I mean it.

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:39.331
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:39.331 --> 00:01:43.002
-Like, I'm seriously a mole.
-Yeah!

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:46.839
It seemed like that
your drama teacher

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:49.809
saw some real talent in you.
-Yes, Mr. Frazel.

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:52.045
-But also was supportive of you.

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:53.179
You talk about the fact that

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:54.914
you were accused of plagiarism
in school.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:56.282
-Yes, during 8th grade,

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:58.718
which I think is such
a pivotal year for kids,

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:01.654
I had
my Western Civilization teacher

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:05.291
accuse me of plagiarism,
and she had no proof,

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:08.328
so she made me go up to
all of my other teachers

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:10.696
and she said, "Do you think
Constance is good enough

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:11.898
to have written this?"

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:14.100
And every teacher said no.
-Oh, my God!

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.704
-Sorry, it makes me cry, even
thinking about it still.

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:20.706
But the only teacher who
said I wrote it

00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:22.308
was my drama teacher,
Mr. Frazel,

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.844
and he said,
"Of course she wrote it."

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:27.547
It's funny, I had been, like,

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:29.415
so focused on the pain
of that story,

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:33.052
it wasn't until I wrote it
in my book that I realized.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.088
I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:37.023
No wonder I ended up
being an actor."

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:39.792
Like, my drama teacher,
Mr. Frazel,

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.394
was the only one
who believed in me.

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:42.762
-Yeah, that's really amazing

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:44.130
to have someone supportive
like that.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.331
-Yeah.
-He was like,

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:48.001
"Would a plagiarist
make a mole this good?"

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.302
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:50.503
He was like,
"I'll tell you this,

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:51.804
there's not a single other
kid in this school

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:53.006
that could have been
a mole like her!"

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.207
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:55.707
-That's the truth.

00:02:55.707 --> 00:02:58.644
-So another thing
you write about

00:02:58.644 --> 00:03:00.779
which received
quite a lot of attention

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:02.115
when it first happened,
which is,

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:03.449
you were on a show,
"Fresh Off the Boat."

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.585
It got renewed, which I think
for most actors,

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:06.753
is a really exciting thing.

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:10.356
And then you publicly sort of
basically shared

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.659
that you were upset that it was
coming back for another year.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:14.126
You received a lot of
criticism for that,

00:03:14.126 --> 00:03:15.495
especially from
people in the business

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:17.697
who would say things like,
"How could an actor ever be

00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:19.232
upset their show got picked up
for another year?"

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:21.167
And so in this book,
you shared what happened,

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.402
which is, you were being
sexually harassed

00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:25.004
by a senior producer.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:29.075
Was it important for you to give
that context

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.143
as to what you were feeling
when you went through that

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:32.378
other public moment?

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:33.812
And how has the response been
for you?

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:36.281
-Well, to be honest, I didn't
want to write about that.

00:03:36.281 --> 00:03:37.917
That was the last essay
I wrote for the book,

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:41.020
and only after being, like,
pushed by my editor,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.388
like,
"You should write about this.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.689
This is what people
want to hear."

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.792
And I was like, I'm done with
that chapter in my life.

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:48.194
Because all of the
sexual harassment,

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:49.495
the inappropriate touching,

00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:51.464
the telling me to
wear short skirts

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:53.165
and intimidation,

00:03:53.165 --> 00:03:57.236
that all only happened in
the first two years of the show

00:03:57.236 --> 00:04:00.506
when I was still very scared
that I --

00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:03.176
You know, I had never done
anything big before.

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:05.211
I had just graduated from
being a waitress,

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:07.480
and I was scared of being fired.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.885
Once I felt a little bit
of job security,

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:15.287
then I started saying no
to this producer,

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:18.057
which infuriated him,
but it was okay.

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:20.425
So I thought, you know what?
I handled it.

00:04:20.425 --> 00:04:23.996
I don't need to stay in
the reputation of this show

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.131
or of this producer.

00:04:26.131 --> 00:04:28.935
I can just keep it inside.

00:04:28.935 --> 00:04:31.403
And as a result, I feel like
I was never able to

00:04:31.403 --> 00:04:33.106
really be myself on set,

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:37.142
'cause I'd see my abuser being
buddy-buddy with everybody else,

00:04:37.142 --> 00:04:40.179
knowing what he had done to me.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:43.482
And, you know,
the thing I learned is that

00:04:43.482 --> 00:04:46.519
bad feelings and abuse
don't just go away

00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:48.120
because you will it to.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.688
It's gonna come out somewhere.

00:04:49.688 --> 00:04:51.524
And so I think -- I mean,

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:54.727
people didn't understand
the context of those tweets.

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:55.895
Um...

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:57.730
And thank you for
not making fun of it,

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.166
because it led me to
a really dark time.

00:05:00.166 --> 00:05:04.069
And so I think I decided
to include -- sorry --

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:07.773
I decided to include it
in the book because, um,

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:12.645
I think it's important that we
engage in curiosity and empathy

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:14.280
before we go straight
to judgement.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.714
Because if somebody
does something

00:05:15.714 --> 00:05:17.083
out of character for them,

00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:19.085
usually something's going on
in their life.

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:20.920
Sorry!
-No, that's alright.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.356
[ Applause ]

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:26.592
-It's really great
that you shared it.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.127
-Yeah?
-And let me just say,

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:31.663
I think it's really special
not just that you gave context,

00:05:31.663 --> 00:05:34.333
but also, it's really important
to share, like

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:35.635
people have jobs to do, right?

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:37.203
And they go to their jobs,
and those jobs,

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:39.238
that should be the hard part, is
doing the job every day.

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:40.640
The extra thing you had to do,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.574
that was an incredibly
unfair burden.

00:05:42.574 --> 00:05:45.578
-And it was before
the Me Too movement, too,

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:47.780
so I was just like,
"Nobody's gonna believe me,"

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.748
except for my middle school
drama teacher.

00:05:49.748 --> 00:05:51.650
[ Laughter ]
You know?

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:54.453
So I just didn't know
what to do.

00:05:54.453 --> 00:05:56.388
-You're like, "There's only
one Mr. Frazel out there!

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:59.292
I'm in real trouble."

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:02.228
Thank you, though, on behalf of
all of us, for sharing it.

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:03.929
It couldn't have been easy.

00:06:03.929 --> 00:06:05.397
Hopefully it feels better.
-It does.

00:06:05.397 --> 00:06:07.100
-Being on the other side of it.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.400
-It's so embarrassing crying.
Sorry.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.302
-That's alright. You know what?

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:11.703
Let's take a break
and we'll come back

00:06:11.703 --> 00:06:13.305
and we'll talk about
"Lyle, Lyle, Crocodile."

00:06:13.305 --> 00:06:15.307
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yes!

00:06:15.307 --> 00:06:18.144
I'm so sorry! I can't help it!

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:20.179
I need a tissue.
-We'll get you a tissue.

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:21.214
We'll be right back.

